##
    The four kinds of number, and which conversions the language makes for you.

    One rule decides all of it: a conversion that loses nothing happens on its own, and one
    that discards information is a decision you write down. Nothing here is about which type
    is better — it is about which question you are asking.

    The short version. An 'integer' counts. A 'real' measures in tens, the way a price or a
    mark does, and it is exact about them. A 'fraction' is a ratio held as two whole numbers,
    so thirds stay thirds. A 'float' is binary floating point — what other languages spell
    'float' or 'double' — and it is here to be learned rather than hidden.
##

shared model Program

    function Main()
        Program.WhatEachHolds();
        Program.WhatConvertsOnItsOwn();
        Program.WhatYouHaveToAskFor();
        Program.WhereFloatDiffers();
    end function

    ##
        @summary: The same number written four ways.

        @remarks: A tenth is the value to watch. Three of these hold it exactly and one of them
        cannot, which is the whole reason the fourth exists as its own type.
    ##
    function WhatEachHolds()
        Console.WriteLine("== what each holds ==");

        integer counted = 3;
        real measured = 0.1;
        fraction ratio = 1|10;
        float binary = 0.1f;

        Console.WriteLine("  integer  " + counted);
        Console.WriteLine("  real     " + measured);
        Console.WriteLine("  fraction " + ratio);
        Console.WriteLine("  float    " + binary);

        # The arithmetic a reader expects, from the type that measures in tens.
        Console.WriteLine("  0.1 + 0.2 == 0.3   -> " + ((0.1 + 0.2) == 0.3));

        # And the same sum in binary, which is the surprise this language moves out of the way.
        Console.WriteLine("  0.1f + 0.2f == 0.3f -> " + ((0.1f + 0.2f) == 0.3f));
    end function

    ##
        @summary: The conversions that need nothing written.

        @remarks: Each of these is exact, so there is nothing to decide and nothing to say.
        A whole number is a real and is a fraction over one; a real counts in tens, so it
        already is a fraction over a power of ten.
    ##
    function WhatConvertsOnItsOwn()
        Console.WriteLine("== what converts on its own ==");

        # An integer, used where a real is wanted.
        real fromWhole = 7;
        Console.WriteLine("  integer -> real     " + fromWhole);

        # An integer, used where a fraction is wanted.
        fraction wholeRatio = 7;
        Console.WriteLine("  integer -> fraction " + wholeRatio);

        # A real, used where a fraction is wanted. Exactly, and this is the interesting one:
        # a tenth becomes the ratio a reader would have written by hand.
        fraction tenth = 0.1;
        fraction quarter = 0.25;
        Console.WriteLine("  real -> fraction    " + tenth + " and " + quarter);

        # Which means the two mix without ceremony, and stay exact through the sum.
        Console.WriteLine("  0.5 + 1|3           " + (0.5 + 1|3));
        Console.WriteLine("  1|3 + 0.25          " + (1|3 + 0.25));

        # Three thirds are one. Doing the same sum in reals would not come back to it.
        Console.WriteLine("  1|3 + 1|3 + 1|3     " + (1|3 + 1|3 + 1|3));
    end function

    ##
        @summary: The conversions you have to ask for, and why.

        @remarks: Neither of these loses information either — every fraction has a decimal
        expansion, and every float is exactly some ratio. They are written out because the
        answer is surprising, and a surprise is worth asking for on purpose.
    ##
    function WhatYouHaveToAskFor()
        Console.WriteLine("== what you have to ask for ==");

        # A third has no exact decimal form, so this is where exactness stops.
        fraction third = 1|3;
        Console.WriteLine("  (1|3).ToReal()      " + third.ToReal());
        Console.WriteLine("  and back to one?    " + ((third.ToReal() * 3) == 1.0));

        # Where the fraction does divide out, nothing is lost and it still has to be asked for:
        # the rule is about the conversion, not about the value that happens to be in it.
        Console.WriteLine("  (1|4).ToReal()      " + (1|4).ToReal());

        # The other one, and the clearest look at what a float really holds.
        Console.WriteLine("  (0.1f).ToFraction() " + (0.1f).ToFraction());
        Console.WriteLine("  (0.5f).ToFraction() " + (0.5f).ToFraction());
    end function

    ##
        @summary: What a float does that a real will not.

        @remarks: A real stops where it runs out, the way an integer does. A float carries on
        into values that are not numbers, and those are what the constants name.
    ##
    function WhereFloatDiffers()
        Console.WriteLine("== where a float differs ==");

        # Dividing by zero produces an infinity rather than stopping, and the zero may be
        # written down: a float is the one type PC0324 leaves alone, because it is the one
        # type with a value for the answer. The same lines written with reals are refused,
        # which is what the negative sample shows.
        Console.WriteLine("  1.0f / 0.0f         " + (1.0f / 0.0f));
        Console.WriteLine("  is it Infinity?     " + ((1.0f / 0.0f) == Float.Infinity));
        Console.WriteLine("  -1.0f / 0.0f        " + (-1.0f / 0.0f));

        # And a value that is not equal to itself, which is worth meeting once on purpose.
        float nothing = 0.0f / 0.0f;
        Console.WriteLine("  0.0f / 0.0f         " + nothing);
        Console.WriteLine("  is it itself?       " + (nothing == nothing));

        # What each type runs out at.
        Console.WriteLine("  largest integer     " + Integer.MaxValue);
        Console.WriteLine("  largest real        " + Real.MaxValue);
        Console.WriteLine("  largest float       " + Float.MaxValue);
    end function

end model
